title: 'C-c U : skg-push-ancestry'
pid: 3d3fd161-04a3-4419-8724-b86386b194d5
body: |2-
  Stores, in the linkstack, the chain of notes from the top of the
  view down to the node the cursor is on, and how each is joined in
  the graph to the one above it. Folder headlines are skipped; the
  forks folder's members, for instance, are joined to its note by a
  fork. Pushed from c in

  : * a
  : ** b
  : *** Its mirrors are these.
  : **** c

  where a contains b and b forks c, the stack holds the tree (each
  headline's ID, its body, left out here)

  : * a
  : ** (aC) b
  : *** (aK) c

  whose child headlines begin with the edge to the note above, written
  as heralds write it, 'a' standing for that note: =aC= it contains
  this one, =Ca= this one contains it; likewise =L= links to, =K= forks
  (=aKa= each forks the other) and =H= hides.

  skg-paste-link (=C-c o l=) pastes it as a chain of links, the top
  first, joined by what joins them:

  : [[id:A][a]] / [[id:B][b]] ~ [[id:C][c]]

  "/" reads "contains" and "\" "is contained by"; "~" forks or is
  forked by; "->" links to and "<-" is linked to by; "hides" and "is
  hidden by" say themselves. Only the last label, c's, is asked for.
  skg-paste-id and skg-paste-node use the last note, c.

  If some note is joined to the one above it by no edge of the graph
  (a mirror joined to the folder's note only through other mirrors,
  say, or a node shown in the place of one it forks), nothing is
  pushed, and Emacs names the two notes. The stack keeps what the
  graph was when you pushed; it does not follow later edits.